//RS //! D-COMPILE-SPEED1 (#3661): one walk shape for structural questions about
//RS //! named types: can a value of this type be cloned, does it own heap data,
//RS //! can it cross a task boundary, does it hold a view, a Cell guard or a
//RS //! `Shared` handle.
//RS //!
//RS //! Each question recurses through struct fields and enum payloads. A walk
//RS //! that only guards the current path re-walks a type once for every path
//RS //! that reaches it, which is exponential in the depth of sharing (every node
//RS //! of a compiler AST reaches `Expr`, `Type` and `Span`), and each binding
//RS //! asked again from scratch. Checking the self-hosted compiler's parser
//RS //! slice spent minutes in those walks.
//RS //!
//RS //! Every such question stops at its first non-neutral answer (a problem
//RS //! found, a field that cannot be cloned), so a named instantiation whose
//RS //! walk finished while the query is still running finished neutral, and a
//RS //! revisit adds nothing: `finished` makes one query linear in the type
//RS //! graph. A type still on the path is assumed neutral (coinduction), as
//RS //! before. When a whole query ends neutral, no problem is reachable from any
//RS //! instantiation it entered, so those instantiations are neutral outright;
//RS //! while body checking runs, the registry is final and its memo keeps them
//RS //! for every later query. Parallel body checkers share one memo: a row only
//RS //! records a proven answer, so the order in which checkers add rows cannot
//RS //! change any answer.
//RS 
